This jellyfish literally swims backward through time. Turritopsis dohrnii, known as the immortal jellyfish, can rewind itself from old age back to infancy and restart its entire life cycle—theoretically making it biologically immortal.

When threatened or stressed, this remarkable creature doesn't die. Instead, it undergoes transdifferentiation, transforming its mature cells back into their younger forms. Its tissues revert to a polyp state, essentially hitting a biological reset button. From there, it grows up again as if born anew. Scientists have observed this process repeating multiple times in the same organism, with no known natural lifespan limit.

This phenomenon challenges everything we know about aging. While humans accumulate cellular damage over time, the immortal jellyfish erases its damage and starts fresh. Researchers studying this creature hope to unlock secrets of cellular rejuvenation that could one day help us understand—and potentially slow—human aging. The immortal jellyfish proves that death isn't inevitable in nature.

